            <html:p>If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> is a
<fr:link href="/sss-00FQ/" title="Locally contractive endofunctors" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FQ/" display-uri="sss-00FQ" type="local">locally contractive</fr:link> endofunctor on presheaves over a
<fr:link href="/sss-00FG/" title="Direct categories" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FG/" display-uri="sss-00FG" type="local">direct category</fr:link>, then every value of the
<fr:link href="/sss-00FV/" title="The coalgebra-to-algebra profunctor" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FV/" display-uri="sss-00FV" type="local">profunctor</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {Hylo}]]></fr:tex> is a singleton: every coalgebra is
<fr:link href="/sss-00FU/" title="Recursive coalgebras and corecursive algebras" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FU/" display-uri="sss-00FU" type="local">recursive</fr:link> and every algebra is corecursive.</html:p>
            <html:p>The proof is one application of <fr:link href="/sss-00FP/" title="Löb induction for presheaves on a direct category" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FP/" display-uri="sss-00FP" type="local">Löb</fr:link>. A hylomorphism from
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha ]]></fr:tex> is a fixed point of
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[h \mapsto  c \star  F h \star  \alpha ]]></fr:tex>
on the presheaf <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X \Rightarrow  B]]></fr:tex> of morphisms. Local contractivity
factors this assignment through <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\rhd {\mathopen {}\left (X \Rightarrow  B\right )\mathclose {}}]]></fr:tex>, so Löb
provides a fixed point and provides it uniquely.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
              <fr:month>7</fr:month>
              <fr:day>19</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FU/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sss-00FU</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/sss-00FU/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Recursive coalgebras and corecursive algebras">Recursive coalgebras and corecursive algebras</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>A <fr:link href="/sss-00F9/" title="Coalgebras as a displayed category" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00F9/" display-uri="sss-00F9" type="local">coalgebra</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c : X \to  F X]]></fr:tex> is <html:em>recursive</html:em> when for
<html:em>every</html:em> <fr:link href="/sss-00F7/" title="Algebras as a displayed category" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00F7/" display-uri="sss-00F7" type="local">algebra</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha  : F B \to  B]]></fr:tex> there is exactly
one <fr:link href="/sss-00FT/" title="Hylomorphisms" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FT/" display-uri="sss-00FT" type="local">hylomorphism</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X \to  B]]></fr:tex>. Recursion out of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> is then
total and deterministic: any way of recombining answers yields a
uniquely-defined function.</html:p>
            <html:p>Recursiveness is the coalgebraic form of well-foundedness. The
coalgebra decomposes each element into subproblems; recursiveness says
this decomposition terminates, without ever mentioning an ordering.
<fr:link href="/alexandruIntrinsicallyCorrectAlgorithms2025-preprint/" title="Intrinsically Correct Algorithms and Recursive Coalgebras" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/alexandruIntrinsicallyCorrectAlgorithms2025-preprint/" display-uri="alexandruIntrinsicallyCorrectAlgorithms2025-preprint" type="local">Alexandru, Urbat, and Wißmann</fr:link>
develop intrinsically correct algorithms from exactly this notion.</html:p>
            <html:p>Dually, an algebra <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha ]]></fr:tex> is <html:em>corecursive</html:em> when every
coalgebra admits exactly one hylomorphism into it. An
<fr:link href="/sss-00F8/" title="Initial algebra" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00F8/" display-uri="sss-00F8" type="local">initial algebra</fr:link> gives a recursive coalgebra (its inverse,
by Lambek's lemma), and a <fr:link href="/sss-00FA/" title="Terminal coalgebra" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-00FA/" display-uri="sss-00FA" type="local">terminal coalgebra</fr:link> gives a
corecursive algebra.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
